<h1>Shuttle &amp; Reciprocating Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A shuttle conveyor is a powered conveyor section mounted on a linear travel mechanism that moves back and forth to deliver products to multiple discharge points from a single infeed. Shuttle systems include shuttle cars (entire conveyor sections riding on tracks), reciprocating pushers (linear actuators that sweep products onto parallel lanes), and pop-up transfer shuttles (chain or roller mechanisms that rise and index between positions). These systems distribute products across palletizer lanes, case packing stations, and warehouse storage lanes in <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging and distribution operations</a>.</p>
<h2 id="shuttle-car-heading">Shuttle Car Systems</h2>
<p>A shuttle car carries an entire powered conveyor section along a linear rail or track system. The car receives product from a fixed infeed conveyor, travels laterally to align with the target discharge lane, and the onboard conveyor drives the product off onto the receiving line. The car then returns to the infeed position for the next product. Shuttle cars serve many parallel lanes from a single infeed point.</p>
<h3>Mechanical Design</h3>
<p>Shuttle cars ride on precision linear rails with recirculating ball bearing guide blocks. A servo or AC gearmotor drives the car through a rack-and-pinion, belt drive, or chain drive. The onboard conveyor is typically a short section of <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/powered/">powered roller</a> or <a href="/conveyor-types/flat-belt-conveyors/">belt conveyor</a> with its own drive motor. Power and control signals reach the moving car through a cable carrier (energy chain), festoon cable system, or bus bar collector.</p>
<p>The car frame is structural steel, sized to support the product load plus the onboard conveyor weight at full travel speed. End-of-travel hard stops and deceleration sensors prevent overtravel. An absolute encoder or laser distance sensor provides real-time position feedback for lane alignment. Specify the alignment tolerance the receiving lane needs and ask the supplier to confirm it.</p>
<h3>Performance Characteristics</h3>
<p>Travel speed, acceleration, and deceleration are controlled by VFD or servo drive, and heavier loads need gentler acceleration. Lane-to-lane cycle time depends on travel distance, so moves to far lanes take much longer than moves to adjacent lanes. Onboard conveyor speed matches the discharge lane speed, which is lower for pallets than for cases.</p>
<p>Throughput calculation accounts for average travel distance, not just adjacent-lane time. If the shuttle serves 10 lanes uniformly, average travel is to lane 5 (half the span). Intelligent sequencing algorithms minimize travel by grouping consecutive products destined for nearby lanes. Ask suppliers whether their controls include lane-assignment sequencing.</p>
<h3>Load Capacity</h3>
<p>Case-handling and pallet-handling shuttle cars are built very differently. The rail system, car frame, and drive sizing scale with the maximum load and travel speed. Heavy pallet shuttle cars use <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR conveyor</a> sections onboard and heavy-duty floor-mounted rail systems. Case shuttle cars use lighter roller or belt conveyor sections and overhead or floor-mounted rails.</p>
<h2 id="pusher-heading">Reciprocating Pushers</h2>
<p>A reciprocating pusher uses a linear actuator (pneumatic cylinder, servo-driven ball screw, or belt-driven linear unit) to push products sideways off a main conveyor line onto one or two perpendicular spur lanes. The pusher plate extends, contacts the product, pushes it across the conveyor width, and retracts. The cycle repeats for the next product.</p>
<h3>Pneumatic Pushers</h3>
<p>Pneumatic reciprocating pushers use air cylinders for the push stroke. They are the simplest and lowest-cost option for diverting products to a single reject lane or alternate between two lanes. Push-and-return cycle time limits how many products per minute a pusher can handle. Push force is air pressure times piston area: a larger bore or higher supply pressure gives more force. Size the cylinder for the heaviest product and its friction on the conveyor surface.</p>
<p>Pneumatic pushers suit <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">reject system</a> applications where checkweighers, metal detectors, and vision systems trigger a push to remove failed products. The actuator fires on command and retracts automatically.</p>
<h3>Servo-Driven Pushers</h3>
<p>Servo-driven reciprocating pushers use a ball screw or belt-driven linear actuator with a servo motor. The servo controls push speed, acceleration, deceleration, and push distance independently. This allows the pusher to handle varying product sizes without mechanical adjustment and to push products gently (soft start, soft stop) to prevent product damage or tipping.</p>
<p>Servo pushers cost more than pneumatic but provide programmable push profiles, position feedback, and the ability to push to several positions from a single actuator by varying the stroke length. They eliminate the compressed air requirement. Cycle time depends on stroke length and product weight.</p>
<h3>Sweep Arm Diverters</h3>
<p>Sweep arm diverters are a variant of reciprocating pushers that use a rotating or pivoting arm instead of a linear pusher plate. The arm sweeps across the conveyor at an angle, guiding the product onto the spur lane with less impact than a straight push. Sweep arms handle orientation-sensitive products better than flat pushers because the angled contact reduces rotation during the divert. See <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/">diverting and merging systems</a>.</p>
<h2 id="popup-heading">Pop-Up Transfer Shuttles</h2>
<p>Pop-up transfer shuttles combine the directional change of a <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">pop-up transfer</a> with the multi-position capability of a shuttle. Chains or rollers rise above the main conveyor surface and drive the product perpendicular to the original travel direction. The pop-up mechanism indexes to different positions along the main conveyor to serve multiple discharge lanes without a moving car.</p>
<h3>Pop-Up Chain Shuttle</h3>
<p>Pop-up chain shuttles use parallel chain strands mounted between <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyor</a> rollers. The chains lift, drive the product sideways, and lower. Multiple pop-up chain sections along the main conveyor each serve a dedicated discharge lane. A PLC controls which pop-up section activates based on product destination. This approach eliminates the moving car entirely but requires one pop-up mechanism per lane.</p>
<p>Pop-up chain transfers handle heavier products than wheel diverters. The product moves sideways without rotating, so a different face leads on the discharge lane. A <a href="https://www.dornerconveyors.com/solutions/pop-up-transfers" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">90-degree pop-up transfer</a>, as Dorner describes it, uses conveyors moving perpendicular to each other to change direction. Pop-up arrays are used in distribution centers where products divert from trunk lines to pick or pack lanes.</p>
<h3>Indexing Pop-Up Transfers</h3>
<p>Indexing pop-up transfers use a single pop-up mechanism that physically moves along the main conveyor to reach different discharge positions. The mechanism rides on a linear rail beneath the conveyor and indexes to the target lane before activating. This reduces the number of pop-up mechanisms needed (one instead of one per lane) but adds index travel time to the cycle.</p>
<p>Indexing pop-up transfers suit lower-throughput applications where the index travel time does not bottleneck the system. They serve several lanes from a single mechanism and cost less than a full shuttle car or a pop-up-per-lane array.</p>
