<h2 id="apps-heading">Shuttle Conveyor Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Palletizer lane distribution:</strong> Shuttle cars distribute cases across multiple palletizer infeed lanes. The shuttle receives cases from a single trunk line and delivers each case to the correct palletizer based on SKU, pallet build sequence, or load-balancing logic. This is the most common shuttle car application in <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging facilities</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Case packing lane feed:</strong> Reciprocating pushers and servo-driven actuators distribute products across a small number of case packing lanes. Each pusher diverts a programmed count of products into the target lane for <a href="/conveyor-functions/collating-laning/">collating and laning</a> before case packing. The servo pusher adjusts stroke length to match lane width and product size.</p>
<p><strong>Warehouse pallet put-away:</strong> Pallet shuttle cars transport pallets from a receiving conveyor to one of many storage lane infeed positions. The shuttle aligns with the target lane and the onboard conveyor pushes the pallet onto the storage lane. This application uses heavy-duty shuttle cars with <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR onboard conveyors</a> rated for the heaviest pallet in the system.</p>
<p><strong>Layer forming and pattern building:</strong> Reciprocating pushers arrange products into layer patterns for palletizing. The pusher moves products into row positions, then a sweep bar pushes the completed row onto the layer-forming table. Multiple push cycles build a complete pallet layer pattern. Servo-driven pushers provide the positioning accuracy needed for tight layer patterns.</p>
<p><strong>Sortation pre-conditioning:</strong> Pop-up transfer shuttle arrays distribute products from trunk lines to <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/sortation/">sortation system</a> infeed lanes. Each pop-up transfer feeds a dedicated sort lane. The PLC activates the correct pop-up based on product destination data from barcode or RFID reads. <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">Accumulation zones</a> upstream of the pop-up array meter products for reliable transfer.</p>
<p><strong>Print-and-apply label stations:</strong> Shuttle conveyors position products at label application stations. The shuttle moves the product under a print-and-apply head, pauses for label placement, and advances to the next station or discharge point. Servo-driven shuttles provide the precise positioning needed for accurate label placement on multiple faces.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">How to Select a Shuttle System</h2>
<p><strong>Number of lanes drives the architecture.</strong> For one or two lanes, reciprocating pushers provide the simplest solution. For a few lanes, servo pushers or small shuttle cars work. For many lanes, full shuttle cars or pop-up transfer arrays are needed. Pop-up arrays scale linearly (add one mechanism per lane) while shuttle cars scale by travel distance (one car covers all lanes).</p>
<p><strong>Throughput determines feasibility.</strong> Calculate required cycles per minute. Products per minute equals 60 divided by the average cycle time in seconds. A shuttle car with a 5-second average cycle delivers 12 products per minute, so a requirement of 30 per minute needs a different approach. Options: add a second shuttle car on parallel rails, switch to a pop-up array, or use a high-speed <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/sortation/">sortation system</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Guard the moving parts.</strong> Shuttle cars, pusher strokes, and pop-up mechanisms create shear and pinch points. <a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.212" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OSHA 29 CFR 1910.212</a> requires guarding of ingoing nip points and other machine hazards, and <a href="https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/b20-1-safety-standard-conveyors-related-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASME B20.1</a> covers conveyor safety. Fence the shuttle travel area or interlock access.</p>
<p><strong>Product weight and fragility set constraints.</strong> Heavy pallets require shuttle cars with structural rail systems. Fragile products require pop-up transfers (no impact) or servo pushers (soft push profiles). Pneumatic pushers suit durable products where impact is acceptable.</p>
<p><strong>Floor space and layout constraints matter.</strong> Shuttle cars need clear travel lanes along the full width of the lane array. Pop-up arrays integrate within the conveyor footprint but require more mechanisms. Reciprocating pushers need clearance on the push side for the actuator stroke.</p>
<p><strong>Integration with upstream systems.</strong> Shuttle conveyors require precise product positioning at the infeed. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyor zones</a> with photo-eye sensors stage products at the shuttle infeed point. An <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation buffer</a> before the shuttle absorbs throughput variations and prevents starvation during long shuttle travel moves.</p>
<h2 id="throughput-heading">Shuttle Throughput Calculations</h2>
<p><strong>Shuttle car throughput formula:</strong></p>
<p class="formula"><strong>Cycle Time = Load Time + Travel Time + Unload Time + Return Time</strong></p>
<p>Load time is the time for product to transfer from the infeed conveyor onto the shuttle car. Travel time depends on distance, car speed, and acceleration. Unload time is the time to discharge product at the target lane. Return time is the travel back to the infeed position.</p>
<p><strong>Illustrative example (assumed inputs, not a rating):</strong> A shuttle car serves 8 lanes spaced 5 feet apart. Car travel speed is 200 FPM (3.33 ft/sec). Average travel distance to target lane is 4 lanes x 5 ft = 20 ft. Load time: 3 sec. Travel to lane: 6 sec. Unload: 3 sec. Return travel: 6 sec. Total cycle: 18 seconds. Maximum throughput: 60/18 = 3.3 products per minute. The example ignores acceleration, so real cycles are longer.</p>
<p><strong>Improving shuttle throughput:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Dual shuttle cars:</strong> Two cars on parallel rails serve alternating cycles and can approach double the throughput if the infeed keeps up.</li>
<li><strong>Intelligent sequencing:</strong> Software sorts the product queue so consecutive products go to nearby lanes, minimizing travel distance.</li>
<li><strong>Multi-product loading:</strong> The shuttle loads more than one product before traveling, serving multiple adjacent lanes per trip.</li>
<li><strong>Bi-directional unload:</strong> The onboard conveyor unloads to either side, halving the average travel distance for some lane configurations.</li>
</ul>
